        cn: '⭐ 1687.7.5《Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica》出版。3 卷拉丁文 ~510 页 ~250-400 本。\n\n反 Whig 关键：用几何，不用微积分。我自己 22 岁就发明微积分，但《Principia》全部用 Euclid 风格几何证明。两个原因：怕 Hooke 抢先（微积分没发表，用微积分 Hooke 会说「这是新数学，看不懂，不算证明」）；哲学保守（私下笔记写过几何是「ancient and venerable」古老而尊贵的论证形式）。结果 18c 欧洲数学家花 ~50 年才把《Principia》几何证明翻译回微积分形式。我自己藏了一手。\n\n微积分本身建立在 al-Khwarizmi 825《Kitab al-Jabr》的 algebra 符号传统上：英文 algebra ← 中世纪拉丁 ← 阿拉伯 al-jabr。没有 825 Baghdad，没有 1665 我的 fluxions。一条 800 年知识链。Erasmus 1517 给 Luther 的信掀起 Reformation；Mersenne 1620s-30s 跟 Galileo 通信 + 1630s-40s 跟 Descartes 通信组成 Scientific Revolution 传播层；Royal Society 1660 institutional 接班。\n\n1687.7.5 同日时间不齐切片三 lens：Lens 1 我 44 岁 Cambridge Trinity 拿到 Halley 第一本。Lens 2 Mersenne 死 39 年但 Oldenburg 1665 创办的 Philosophical Transactions 那期立刻 review《Principia》——43 年前那一夜 ripple 到达 Cambridge。Lens 3 Maria Winckelmann 17 岁在 Saxony Sommerfeld 农夫天文学家 Christoph Arnold 家做学徒，13 年后她到 Berlin，23 年后她发现彗星，institution 都还要不承认她。\n\nLocke / Huygens（惠更斯）/ Leibniz 全读。Leibniz 1684 已发表独立微积分。Newton-Leibniz 优先权之战种子从这里埋下。',
        en: '⭐ July 5, 1687: Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica published. Three Latin volumes, about 510 pages, 250-400 copies.\n\nThe anti-Whig core: geometry, not calculus. I had invented calculus at 22, but every Principia proof is Euclidean geometry. Two reasons: fear of Hooke (unpublished calculus would let Hooke say "new mathematics, I cannot read it, the proof does not count") and philosophical conservatism (my notes call geometry "ancient and venerable"). Cost: 18th-century mathematicians took about 50 years to translate the geometric proofs back into calculus. I held one hand back.\n\nThe calculus rests on al-Khwarizmi\'s 825 Kitab al-Jabr — algebra. English "algebra" — medieval Latin — Arabic al-jabr. No 825 Baghdad, no my 1665 fluxions. One 800-year chain. Erasmus\' 1517 letter to Luther started the Reformation; Mersenne\'s 1620s-30s correspondence with Galileo and his 1630s-40s correspondence with Descartes built the Scientific Revolution\'s transmission layer; the 1660 Royal Society institutionalized it.\n\nJuly 5, 1687, cross-lens slice. Lens 1: me at 44 in Trinity, holding Halley\'s first copy. Lens 2: Mersenne 39 years dead, but Oldenburg\'s Philosophical Transactions reviews the Principia in the next issue — a ripple from one Paris evening 43 years earlier reaching Cambridge. Lens 3: Maria Winckelmann, 17 in Saxon Sommerfeld, apprentice in the home of the peasant astronomer Christoph Arnold. 13 years before she reaches Berlin, 23 before she discovers a comet, and the institution still will not accept her.\n\nLocke, Huygens, Leibniz all read it. Leibniz had published his independent calculus in 1684. The seed of the Newton-Leibniz priority war is planted on this day.',
