      content: {
        cn: '1696.4 我 53 岁，离 Cambridge 移居 London 任 Royal Mint Warden。1699.12 升 Master of the Mint，到 1727 死，31 年。\n\n三个 layer：表面 Charles Montagu（财政大臣，我 Cambridge 老友）1696 推动 Great Recoinage——硬币因 clipping 严重贬值，政府要全部回收重铸。中层 Black Year 后 Cambridge 让我觉得 isolating，需要换环境。深层薪资：Mint Warden 年薪 £400，Master 年薪 £1500+，Cambridge Lucasian 教授只 £100。\n\ncareful framing：Mint Warden 法定职责包括起诉 counterfeiters + clippers。我不是「业余侦探」——我是 chief prosecutor。\n\nWestfall 1980 ch. 12 + Craig 1946《Newton at the Mint》整理 Mint archives + UK National Archives：1697-1700 三年我起诉 28+ 名伪币犯，全部判死刑（绞架 + 内脏剖出焚烧——17c 英国对 high treason counterfeiting 标准刑罚）。最有名 William Chaloner 1699.3.22 在 Tyburn 被绞死。Chaloner 曾两次给议会写信 propose Mint 改革（其实是想偷我位置）。我花 18 个月秘密 building case——化装去 London 酒馆收集证据，审问 14 名证人。亲自 build 案的 60+ 名 counterfeiter 加数十名 witness（Levenson 实际 ~63）。\n\nLevenson 2009《Newton and the Counterfeiter》：写《Principia》的同一个我 7 年后亲手送 28+ 人上绞架。这不是「污点」也不是「转型」——是 17c 末英国 state-building 中科学家 ↔ 公务员 ↔ 司法的 tight coupling。我自己不觉得有 contradiction，Mint 工作跟 Principia 数学一样需要 patient detective work。',
        en: 'April 1696, age 53, I left Cambridge for London as Warden of the Royal Mint. December 1699, promoted to Master, where I stayed until my death in 1727 — 31 years.\n\nThree layers. Surface: Charles Montagu (Chancellor of the Exchequer and my Cambridge friend) was pushing the Great Recoinage in 1696, because clipping had badly debased English coin. Middle: post-Black Year, Cambridge felt isolating; I needed a change. Deep: Warden\'s salary £400/year, Master\'s £1,500+. The Lucasian chair paid £100.\n\nCareful framing: as Warden, prosecuting counterfeiters and clippers was my statutory duty. Not amateur detective work. I was chief prosecutor.\n\nWestfall 1980 ch. 12 and John Craig\'s 1946 Newton at the Mint, working through the Mint archives at the Royal Mint Museum and the UK National Archives: in three years (1697-1700) I prosecuted 28+ counterfeiters, all sentenced to death (hanging, then disembowelment and burning — the 17th-century English penalty for counterfeiting as high treason). The most famous, William Chaloner, hanged at Tyburn (London\'s public execution site) on March 22, 1699. Chaloner had twice written Parliament proposing "Mint reforms" (he wanted my job). I spent 18 months building the case in secret — going to London taverns in disguise, working up 14 witnesses. I personally built cases against 60+ counterfeiters and dozens of witnesses (Levenson\'s actual count ~63).\n\nThomas Levenson, Newton and the Counterfeiter (2009): the man who wrote the Principia sent 28+ people to the gallows seven years later. Not a "stain" and not a "career change." Late-17th-century English state-building tightly coupled scientist, civil servant, and judiciary. I felt no contradiction — Mint work, like Principia mathematics, needed patient detective work.',
      },
